fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1085", description: some(value: "`validate_arrow_param_result` COMPARES TWO PARAMETER LISTS BY NAME WHERE THEY MUST ALIGN POSITIONALLY — and a groundness gate has been hiding the disagreement rather than deferring it.\n\nThe function serves BOTH callable spellings through one comparison. Its param check is `types_compatible(d_param, a_param)`, and its own comment says why: \"WI-775: BY NAME. `arrow_parts` decomposes a `Function[A = …]` too, so a positional bridge here would reopen the hole on that surface; the genuine arrow-vs-arrow param relation is `arrow_compatible_view`'s, which does align positionally.\" That is exactly right about `Function[A = …]`, whose `A` is ONE argument's data type, and exactly wrong about a genuine arrow-vs-arrow pair, whose param slot at arity != 1 IS a parameter LIST — applied positionally, with `_1.._n` synthetic (WI-782: \"`(acc: A, x: B) -> R` accepts a value typed `(_1: A, _2: B) -> R`, which is what lets a named-binder callback take an operation's eta-expanded arrow\").\n\nMEASURED ON WI-1084, and this is what makes it a defect rather than a reading: that ticket σ-resolved the param before the groundness test, which makes both sides ground for an ORDINARY higher-order call and lets the comparison actually run. 38 tests fail, all with the same shape — `expected (acc: ?Acc, x: Element), got (_1: Int64, _2: Int64)` at `foldLeft.f` / `foldRight.f` — across WI-064/278/411/424/492/493/585 and the stdlib combinators. So the comparison is wrong for the arrow-vs-arrow population and is reached today only when it happens to be skipped. WI-1084 therefore resolved only the RESULT and left the param as written, recording this at the site.\n\nTHE CORRECT RELATION ALREADY EXISTS, TWICE, so this is a routing fix and not a new judgement: `unify_arrow_params` selects `TupleAlign::PARAM_LIST` at arity != 1 when both sides are named tuples (the UNIFY side), and `named_tuple_compatible_as` takes the same alignment mode (the SUBTYPE side). `arrow_compatible_view` already uses the positional relation for a real arrow pair. What `validate_arrow_param_result` lacks is the ability to TELL WHICH SPELLING it is looking at — `arrow_parts` deliberately maps both onto one `param`, erasing the distinction the choice depends on. The discriminator is `type_dispatch_name_view(declared)`: `\"arrow\"` vs `\"parameterized\"`.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS BEYOND TIDINESS. Today the param position of a NON-GROUND callable argument is checked by nothing at all: the whole-type `types_compatible` is withheld one frame up (`validate_arg_against_param`'s `type_contains_callable` carve-out, WI-836), and this component check skips a non-ground component. So a polymorphic callback whose PARAMETER type disagrees is accepted, the mirror of the RESULT-position hole WI-1084 closed. The two were one defect with two components; only the result half has been fixed.\n\nSCOPE: split the two readings in `validate_arrow_param_result` — positional `TupleAlign::PARAM_LIST` alignment when the declared side is an `arrow` at arity != 1, by-name when it is a `Function[A = …]` (or arity 1, where the slot is a TYPE and a tuple there is DATA — WI-791). THEN σ-resolve the param through the substitution as WI-1084 did for the result, which is the step that makes the check reachable at all, and MEASURE what it newly refuses: the 38 rows above must go green, and a genuine param disagreement for a polymorphic argument must become a refusal.\n\nACCEPTANCE: a program passing a callback whose PARAMETER type contradicts the declared slot is refused where the argument is polymorphic, driven with its monomorphic twin beside it (which is refused today) so the row measures the change and not the rule; all 38 named rows stay green, and `wi787_eta_spread_named_tuple_test` / `wi784_closure_arity_test` / `wi775*` / `wi792*` keep their verdicts; `validate_arrow_param_result` no longer decides two different questions with one comparison, and says at the site which spelling takes which relation; a test states which case fails when each half is backed out separately; every seven-tier corpus load keeps its fact/rule totals. cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nREFERENCE: WI-1084 (where this was measured — the σ-resolution that exposed it, and the result-half fix that is its twin), WI-775 (what `Function`'s `A` means, and the crash that made the by-name rule right THERE), WI-782 (the `_1.._n` convention and the positional zip), WI-791 (arity selects WHICH relation applies — the same discriminator this needs), WI-836 (the `type_contains_callable` carve-out one frame up that withholds the whole-type check), WI-442/WI-790 (`TupleAlign::PARAM_LIST` and what makes a label synthetic)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-12T13:05:57Z"))
